# Canonical contributor identities for discretus.
#
# Git reads this file to merge the several names and addresses one person may
# have committed with into a single identity. It changes what the reporting
# commands display and leaves the commit objects untouched, so editing this
# file never rewrites history and never invalidates a clone, a tag, or a
# signature.
#
# Commands that honour the mapping:
#
#   git shortlog --summary --numbered --email
#   git log --author="Olaf"
#   git log --format='%aN <%aE>'
#   git blame --line-porcelain
#   git check-mailmap "Some Name <some@address>"
#
# Commands that do not honour it, because they read the raw object:
#
#   git cat-file commit HEAD
#   git log --format='%an <%ae>'          # lower case, raw author
#
# The distinction matters when auditing authorship. The upper case
# placeholders %aN and %aE apply the mapping; the lower case %an and %ae
# report what is actually stored in the commit.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One mapping per line, in any of four forms:
#
#   Canonical Name <canonical@address>
#       Fix the display name for every commit made with that address.
#
#   <canonical@address> <other@address>
#       Fold a second address into the canonical one, keeping the name that
#       each commit already carries.
#
#   Canonical Name <canonical@address> <other@address>
#       Fold a second address into the canonical one and normalize the name
#       at the same time. This is the form used most often.
#
#   Canonical Name <canonical@address> Old Name <other@address>
#       Match on the old name as well, which is required when two people
#       once shared an address, for example a shared laboratory account.
#
# Lines beginning with a hash are comments. Matching is case insensitive on
# the address and exact on the name.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Add a line here rather than asking a contributor to rewrite their history.
# A rewrite changes every commit hash from the edit onward, breaks the links
# in issues and reviews that reference those hashes, and invalidates any
# signature the contributor made, all in exchange for a cosmetic change that
# this file achieves for free.
#
# The canonical address for the author is the forge account address, because
# that is the address the forge links commits to when it builds a
# contribution profile. Institutional and personal addresses are folded into
# it. The institutional address remains the correspondence address and is
# recorded in AUTHORS.md, CITATION.cff, codemeta.json, and the contact
# section of the README, which are the places a reader looks for it.
#
# A contributor whose name appears incorrectly in the release statistics
# should open a pull request that adds a line here. Such a change needs no
# discussion and is merged as soon as it is verified with:
#
#   git check-mailmap "Reported Name <reported@address>"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mappings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Author and maintainer. The account address is canonical, and the
# institutional address at the University of Eastern Finland is folded into
# it so that the history reports one contributor rather than two.
Olaf Yunus Laitinen Imanov <olaf.laitinen@gmail.com>
Olaf Yunus Laitinen Imanov <olaf.laitinen@gmail.com> <yimanov@student.uef.fi>

# Automated accounts. Listing them keeps the dependency and workflow update
# commits from appearing as several distinct contributors in the release
# statistics, and it makes the human contribution count meaningful.
dependabot[bot] <49699333+dependabot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
dependabot[bot] <49699333+dependabot[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> <support@dependabot.com>
github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verification
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# After editing, confirm that the history collapses to the identities you
# expect, and that no unintended address was folded in:
#
#   git shortlog --summary --numbered --email
#   git log --format='%aN <%aE>' | sort -u
#
# The second command should print one line per real contributor plus one
# line per automated account.
